All three parts are built and tested, and I found the cause of the `first-user-evals` span error. The SQL below has not been run against your Supabase project, and the eval set has not been re-run.

## SQL to run
Run this in the Supabase SQL editor. It is the last block of `supabase/schema.sql` and is safe to re-run. Until it is run, queueing a run from the web form fails, because the new `enqueue_web_run` does not exist yet.

```sql
alter table public.runs add column if not exists access_mode text
  check (access_mode in ('public', 'link', 'account', 'signup'));
alter table public.runs add column if not exists findings jsonb;

drop function if exists public.enqueue_web_run(text, text, text, integer, integer, text, text, text);

create or replace function public.enqueue_web_run(
  p_url text, p_goal text, p_ip_hash text, p_max_queued integer, p_max_per_hour integer,
  p_start_url text default null, p_test_email text default null, p_test_password text default null,
  p_access_mode text default null
)
returns jsonb
language plpgsql
security invoker
set search_path = ''
as $$
declare
  queued_count integer;
  recent_count integer;
  oldest_recent timestamptz;
  new_id uuid;
  with_login boolean := p_test_email is not null and p_test_password is not null;
begin
  perform pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext('public.enqueue_web_run'));
  select count(*) into queued_count from public.runs where status = 'queued';
  if queued_count >= p_max_queued then
    return jsonb_build_object('error', 'queue_full');
  end if;
  select count(*), min(created_at) into recent_count, oldest_recent
  from public.runs where ip_hash = p_ip_hash and created_at > now() - interval '1 hour';
  if recent_count >= p_max_per_hour then
    return jsonb_build_object('error', 'rate_limited',
      'retry_after_seconds', ceil(extract(epoch from (oldest_recent + interval '1 hour' - now())))::integer);
  end if;
  insert into public.runs (url, goal, source, ip_hash, start_url, has_test_login, access_mode)
  values (p_url, p_goal, 'web', p_ip_hash, p_start_url, with_login, p_access_mode)
  returning id into new_id;
  if with_login then
    insert into public.run_secrets (run_id, test_email, test_password)
    values (new_id, p_test_email, p_test_password);
  end if;
  return jsonb_build_object('id', new_id);
end;
$$;

revoke execute on function public.enqueue_web_run(text, text, text, integer, integer, text, text, text, text)
  from public, anon, authenticated;
grant execute on function public.enqueue_web_run(text, text, text, integer, integer, text, text, text, text)
  to service_role;
```

## Part 1: login choice on the form
- **The choice:** "How should the visitor get in?" has the four options and the notes you specified. Only the chosen option's fields exist in the form, and the API ignores fields from other options. The Google/GitHub line is there.
- **Worker:** the choice is saved as `runs.access_mode` and told to the visitor in its first message. Only "sign up by itself" lets `sign_up` create an account; in the other modes it refuses and nothing is made.
- **Verified:** in a real browser against a fake database, each option shows exactly its fields and posts exactly its fields, and an empty link or incomplete account is refused before any request.

## Part 2: sign-in diagnosis
- **Outcomes:** after `sign_in` or `sign_up`, the result is one of signed in, rejected, needs verification, or no effect. Rejected quotes the page's message and any 401, 403 or 429 from its own requests. Needs verification covers an email code, a CAPTCHA or two-factor.
- **Blocked runs:** if the visitor is not in, the run ends as blocked with that exact reason and evidence (step, URL, message, statuses). The same credentials are not submitted again: a rejection or verification step ends the visit, and "no effect" gets one retry.
- **Login on the page:** a login printed on the page is detected and reported, and "No login was used" no longer appears when one was used.
- **Report section:** "How the visitor got in" now quotes the diagnosis, for example: *The page rejected the test login (step 4): "Invalid email or password". The page's own request returned 401.*
- **Verified:** 17 unit tests, plus live scenarios with the real model and browser. Those covered a rejection with a 401 (one submission only), CAPTCHA, two-factor, a button that does nothing, and a demo login on the page. A real saucedemo visit reported the page login correctly.
- **Limit:** statuses are only seen for `fetch` and XHR requests. A plain HTML form post that returns 401 shows the message but not the status.

## Part 3: findings with fix prompts
- **What it writes:** a traced `report_writer` step writes at most five findings, ranked by how likely each is to stop a new user. Each has the fields you listed, including a kind and a paste-ready fix prompt.
- **What code enforces:** a finding must point at a step that exists, or it is dropped. The screenshot and the console error or failed request come from that step. Steps to reproduce are the visitor's own recorded actions. Quotes must appear in the record.
- **Our tool's failures:** failures of our own browser tool never become findings. Those steps are shown to the model as "not evidence about the site", and findings pointing at them are dropped.
- **Report page:** findings sit after the verdict and first impression, each with a "Copy fix prompt" button. In a real browser the clipboard got the prompt, the step link works, and there is no horizontal scroll at 390 px.
- **Quality fixes:** the first real run padded two landing-page points that repeat the first-impression review, so I told the writer not to repeat it. It also misread the `[test email]` redaction label as placeholder text, and a plain rejected login was reported as a site problem. A clean saucedemo visit now gives 0 findings; the report step costs about $0.0003–0.0005 per visit.
- **Safety:** the fix prompt is built from untrusted page text and will be pasted into another agent, so it always marks quoted page text as data, not instructions.

## Why `first-user-evals` showed an error
`evals/run.py` had no root span, so neatlogs invented a `first-user-evals` workflow root and exported it with status `UNSET` while every span under it was `OK`. The dashboard shows that as an error. I reproduced this with the exporter log (root `UNSET`, children `OK`). `main()` is now an explicit WORKFLOW span, which exports `OK` (or `ERROR` if the run raises) and puts all judge calls in one trace. I confirmed this with a reproduction, not by running the full eval set.

## Verification
- 128 tests pass (19 live ones skipped). The live end-to-end set, 16 checks over 9 scenarios, passes against a harness run.
- A trace check on saucedemo gave one trace with no orphans or error spans, and `report_writer` present.
- The secret scan finds 0 matches. Web typecheck and lint are clean.

## Not done
- **Eval set:** the eval set has not been re-run since these changes.
- **Schema fallback:** the run page falls back to the older columns if the SQL has not been run, so it will not break in the meantime.
- **Commit:** `hackathon.md` and `NEATLOGS_NOTES.md` are updated, and nothing is committed.

